---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Debt and Leverage — How Much Is Too Much";
const description =
  "Debt-to-equity, interest coverage and net debt to EBITDA: what each ratio " +
  "measures, why coverage matters more than the debt level, and sector context.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Fundamentals / Debt and leverage">
  <h1>Debt and leverage</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Debt is not inherently bad. It is cheaper than equity, and interest is
    tax-deductible, so a moderate amount usually raises returns for shareholders.
    What makes it dangerous is that the obligation is fixed while the profit that
    services it is not.
  </p>

  <h2>The three ratios worth knowing</h2>
  <div class="formula">Debt to equity      = Total borrowings / Shareholders' equity
Interest coverage   = EBIT / Interest expense
Net debt to EBITDA  = (Total debt − Cash) / EBITDA</div>

  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>debt_to_equity</code><span class="meta">how it is funded</span></div>
      <p>The most quoted, and the least informative on its own. It describes the
        structure but says nothing about whether the company can afford it. A
        utility at 2× may be far safer than a cyclical manufacturer at 0.8×.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>interest_coverage</code><span class="meta">can it pay?</span></div>
      <p><strong>The one that matters most.</strong> How many times over does
        operating profit cover the interest bill. Below roughly 2× there is very
        little margin for a bad year; below 1× the company is not earning enough
        to pay its lenders from operations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>net_debt_to_ebitda</code><span class="meta">how long to repay</span></div>
      <p>Roughly how many years of operating earnings it would take to clear the
        debt, netting off cash. Lenders and rating agencies lean on this one
        heavily.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why coverage beats the debt level</h2>
  <p>
    A debt-to-equity of 1.5 tells you the shape of the balance sheet. Interest
    coverage tells you whether that shape is survivable. Consider two illustrative
    companies, both at 1.5× debt-to-equity:
  </p>
  <div class="formula">Company A: EBIT ₹500, interest ₹60   → coverage 8.3×
Company B: EBIT ₹500, interest ₹400  → coverage 1.25×</div>
  <p class="lede">
    Identical leverage ratios. Company A can absorb a 40% fall in profit and still
    pay. Company B cannot absorb a 20% fall. The difference is the interest rate
    it borrowed at — which itself reflects what lenders already think of its risk.
  </p>

  <h2>Sector context</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>utilities_infrastructure</code><span class="meta">high debt is normal</span></div>
      <p>Predictable, often regulated cash flows support leverage that would be
        reckless elsewhere. Judge against sector norms, not an absolute number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>cyclicals</code><span class="meta">debt is dangerous</span></div>
      <p>Metals, real estate, capital goods. Revenue can halve in a downturn while
        interest does not move. Leverage that looked comfortable at the top of the
        cycle becomes fatal at the bottom.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>banks_and_nbfcs</code><span class="meta">different framework entirely</span></div>
      <p>Borrowing is the raw material of lending, so these ratios do not apply.
        Lenders are assessed on capital adequacy, asset quality and provisioning
        instead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>asset_light_services</code><span class="meta">debt-free is common</span></div>
      <p>IT services and similar businesses often carry net cash. Any meaningful
        debt in such a company is worth understanding, because the business model
        does not require it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What the headline number hides</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>short_term_borrowings</code><span class="meta">refinancing risk</span></div>
      <p>Debt maturing within a year must be repaid or rolled over. A company
        dependent on continuous refinancing is exposed to credit markets tightening
        at exactly the wrong moment.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>contingent_liabilities</code><span class="meta">off the balance sheet</span></div>
      <p>Guarantees given for subsidiaries, disputed tax demands, pending
        litigation. Not counted as debt, but they can become debt. They are in the
        notes to accounts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>related_party_lending</code><span class="meta">read the notes</span></div>
      <p>Loans and guarantees to group entities can move obligations around so
        that no single entity looks stretched. Consolidated statements are the ones
        to read.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>promoter_pledging</code><span class="meta">not company debt, still your risk</span></div>
      <p>Promoters borrowing against their own shareholding does not appear on the
        company's balance sheet at all, but a margin call forces selling and
        collapses the price. See
        <a href="/learn/promoter-governance/">promoter holding and governance</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The link to returns</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Leverage flatters <a href="/learn/roe-roce/">ROE</a> without improving ROCE,
    because it changes the funding rather than the operation. The test is simple:
    if ROCE is comfortably above the cost of borrowing, debt is adding value to
    shareholders. If ROCE is below it, every additional rupee borrowed makes them
    poorer, and growth accelerates the damage.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>What debt does to a share price</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Leveraged companies move more violently in both directions, because the equity
    absorbs all of the variation in profit while the interest bill stays fixed.
    That shows up on a chart as higher volatility — you will see it in the
    <span class="c">atr_pct</span> column of the
    <a href="/screener/">screener</a>. The indicator measures the symptom; the
    balance sheet explains the cause.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/cash-flow/">cash flow vs profit</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/roe-roce/">ROE and ROCE</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/evaluating-a-business/">evaluating a business</a>
  </p>
</Base>
